{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context\n\nFound by the Step-7 parity sweep of bug `intangible-ladyish-vicuna` (`93a9-66cf-e681-4f49`),\nwhich restored the compaction trigger's sidecars to the canonical store.\n\n`doctor_locks.scan_locks` (`src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py`) canonicalises the tracker\n(`_lock.canonical_tracker`) and then reports four legs — `tickets-write-fcntl`,\n`tickets-write-mkdir`, `hlc`, `enrich-drain`. There is NO leg for `compact-worker.lock`, even\nthough it is a stamped advisory lock in the same `.rebar/`\n(`compact_trigger._TRIGGER_LOCK_NAME = \"compact-worker.lock\"`, derived from the CANONICAL\ntracker per fix 13f78e3165 / `intangible-ladyish-vicuna`), arbitrated by the same\n`lock_owner.stamped_file_is_stale` decision table as the drain lock next to it.\n\nConsequence: a compaction worker that dies between acquire and release leaves a stamped lock\nthat `rebar doctor` cannot see, so the one diagnostic built to answer \"who holds what\" is\nsilent about the lock most likely to be held by a detached background process. (The lock IS\nself-healing via the shared staleness table, so this is observability, not correctness.)\n\nNote: the `intangible-ladyish-vicuna` description asserted that \"`rebar doctor` describes the\ncanonical `compact-worker.lock`\". It does not — that assumption was checked and disproved\nduring the debug session, which is what surfaced this gap.\n\n## Scope\n\nAdd a `compact-worker` leg to `scan_locks` alongside `enrich-drain`, reusing the existing\nstamped-file report helper `_existence_report` (the compact-worker lock is the same\n`O_EXCL`-file-with-v2-stamp shape as the drain lock — `compact_trigger` reuses the drain's\nmachinery by design). Deliberately NOT done inside the bug fix: adding a doctor leg is\nnew capability, and that fix was held to restoration only.\n\nOut of scope: snapshot GC's `<store>/gc/worker.lock` (a3da2f4cce) lives in the snapshot\nstore, not the tracker's `.rebar/`, and gets its own discovered_from ticket rather than\nscope-creeping here.\n\n## Plan (exact edit set)\n\nIn `src/rebar/_commands/doctor_locks.py`:\n1. Add `LEG_COMPACT_WORKER = \"compact-worker\"` beside the other leg-name constants and\n   `_COMPACT_WORKER_LOCK_NAME = \"compact-worker.lock\"` beside `_DRAIN_LOCK_NAME` (kept as a\n   local constant, matching how `_DRAIN_LOCK_NAME` mirrors `enrich_drain`'s name rather than\n   importing across packages).\n2. Add a fifth leg tuple to `scan_locks`, directly after `enrich-drain`:\n   `_existence_report(LEG_COMPACT_WORKER, os.path.join(rebar_dir, _COMPACT_WORKER_LOCK_NAME),\n   note=...)`. `rebar_dir` is already derived from the CANONICALISED tracker at the top of\n   `scan_locks`, so the leg inherits the canonical-store keying the compact trigger itself\n   uses (fix 13f78e3165) with no new path logic.\n3a. Admit the new leg in `src/rebar/schemas/doctor.schema.json` — the doctor output\n   schema enumerates the lock-leg names, so the census gains `compact-worker` there too\n   (surfaced by the schema-validation test in CI).\n3. Update the module docstring's leg census (\"Four legs are reported\" → five, naming\n   `.rebar/compact-worker.lock` as a second stamped existence lock).\n\nIn `tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py` (the doctor-locks test module — the\n`tests/unit/` path originally recorded on this ticket does not exist):\n4. Extend `test_a_free_store_reports_every_leg_and_no_finding` to include\n   `LEG_COMPACT_WORKER` in the expected leg-name set and assert its state is `free` with no\n   holder (the free store must not report a spurious holder).\n5. Add held-leg tests mirroring the drain-lock ones, via a `_plant_compact_lock` helper\n   parallel to `_plant_drain_lock`:\n   - a live-holder compact-worker lock reads `held`, names this pid, `pid_state == \"live\"`,\n     `not-stale`, and produces NO finding;\n   - a dead-holder compact-worker lock reads `stale` and IS a `stale-lock` finding.\n\n## Testing / Verification\n\n- RED first: the new/extended tests fail against the current four-leg census (missing leg),\n  then GREEN after the edit.\n- `pytest tests/scripts/graph/test_doctor_locks.py -q` green.\n- `make lint`, `make typecheck`, `python scripts/check_complexity_baseline.py --check` green\n  (`scan_locks` grows one data tuple, not a branch, so its McCabe score is unchanged).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] `rebar doctor` reports a `compact-worker` lock leg, on the canonical store, with the\n      same held-for / staleness / holder detail the `enrich-drain` leg carries.\n- [x] A test covers a held compact-worker lock appearing in the census (live holder → info,\n      dead holder → stale finding).\n- [x] A test covers a free store not reporting a spurious compact-worker holder.\n- [x] `make lint`, `make typecheck`, complexity ratchet and the unit sweep green."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787358793435495001,"uuid":"14ab50af-0b17-4630-a7a4-997adaec1907"}